  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nClouds will continue to stream over the area as SW flow pushes\na warmer and more moist airmass into the area. A few isolated\nradar returns have popped up, but surface moisture is too low to\nallow for any precipitation to reach the ground outside of a few\nsprinkles over higher terrain. Cold air remains trapped in the\nMohawk Valley, with current temps in the mid 40s while much of\nthe area to the south is in the low to mid 50s. Temps will\ncontinue to warm through the afternoon, with highs in the low \nto mid 60s across the Finger Lakes and upper 50s to low 60s \nacross the rest of the region.\n\nMostly sunny skies should prevail on Thursday with southwest \nwinds increasing between 7-15 mph. This combined with 850mb \ntemperatures reaching +11C will allow for high temperatures in \nthe upper 60s to mid-70s, which is about 15 degrees above \naverage for the end of October. \n\nA weak frontal boundary approaches Thursday evening into Thursday \nnight from the north. This will bring increasing clouds and a few \nshowers; mainly along and north of the Southern Tier, with the\nbest chance for a few hundreths of an inch along the I-90\ncorridor. Rain should dissipate before moving into the Twin \nTiers and NEPA. After the dry cold front moves through there \nshould be lake effect clouds over Central NY through much of\nFriday. Breezy NW winds with gusts up to 25 mph are expected\nacross the region. The clouds and NW flow will bring cooler\ntemps, with highs in the mid 50s to low 60s, which is still \nslightly above average for this time of year. High pressure \nbuilds overhead Friday night through Sunday. This will set the \nstage for a seasonable, dry and exceptional fall weather \nweekend.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA pattern shift looks to occur next week, with guidance showing\na chance for a return of showers sometime in the middle of next\nweek. The synoptic setup is quite intricate, with multiple\ntroughs moving into the west coast at the beginning of the week.\nHow these troughs phase and move is still very uncertain, while\na strong ridge of high pressure over eastern Canada will also\nhave a say in how quickly the weather system moves eastward or\nif it tracks into the Great Lakes or is pushed into the SE US.\nConfidence in the specifics of the system is low, but we have\nenough guidance showing a system developing to mention the\nchance for rain next week.",
